  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed explanation of why \"pure\" gold is 99.9% and not 100%:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd2c Why Is \"Pure\" Gold 99.9% and Not 100%?\n\n#### 1. **100% Pure Gold Is Physically Impossible**\nBoth 24-karat and 999.9 fineness are generally accepted as pure gold. Despite this, in truth it is impossible to produce 100% pure gold based on current technical limits.\n\nPerfect purity would mean a piece of gold has zero non-gold atoms. But nature has its own plans \u2014 pure gold naturally has some other atoms mingling in there. Plus, these so-called \"impurities\" actually have a job: they help stabilize the metal's structure.\n\n#### 2. **Limitations of the Refining Process**\nRefining metals to remove impurities costs money. There's no benefit in making gold bullion used for monetary or jewelry means any better than 99.99%; doing so just raises the cost with no real benefit.\n\nNo matter how advanced the refining technology, there will always be trace amounts of other elements remaining in the gold. It is currently impossible to refine gold to a purity of 100% (1,000 fineness). 999.999 fineness is the purest gold ever produced, and proved too expensive to be a regular occurrence.\n\n#### 3. **How Purity Is Measured \u2014 The Millesimal Fineness Scale**\nGold's purity is graded based on the millesimal fineness scale. This scale determines the purity of a precious metal by measuring the number of parts per thousand of pure metal. Typically, anything that is 99.9% gold (999) or more is considered fine gold.\n\nThe purest gold commercially available is 999.99, also called \"five nines\" fine. Just below this is 999.9 gold, also called \"four nines\" fine.\n\n#### 4. **99.9% Is an Industry Standard, Not a Flaw**\nIn the world of gold, \"pure gold\" refers specifically to 24-carat gold, which is typically labeled as 999 (99.9% pure). This is simply the agreed-upon industry standard for what counts as \"pure.\"\n\nGenerally, the closest to 100% pure gold that refiners are able to reach in everyday production is a fineness of 999.9 gold. Anything at or above 999 gold is still considered pure, and would fetch the same price when selling it back to a dealer.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfc5 Summary Table\n\n| Purity Label | % Pure | Common Name |\n|---|---|---|\n| 999 | 99.9% | Three nines / Standard pure gold |\n| 999.9 | 99.99% | Four nines fine |\n| 999.99 | 99.999% | Five nines fine (rarest/most expensive) |\n\nIn short, **gold is never truly 100% pure** because trace impurities are naturally present and virtually impossible to fully eliminate with any current refining technology. The 99.9% (or higher) standard is simply the closest humanity can practically and economically get to perfection.",
